
`native/` holds the AUnit tests for ImplementRooier/InterfaceRooier
(../lib/RooierbesturingCore/src/), run natively (no Teensy attached):

- `native/tests/` -- one `test_<ClassName>.cpp` per class under test.
- `native/support/` -- a small, self-contained set of Arduino-shaped stubs
  (Print/Stream/EEPROM/Arduino.h/etc.), not a real EpoxyDuino dependency, plus
  `native_main.cpp` (the host `int main()` entry point). No `VehicleGps.hpp`
  stub here -- GPS was dropped for this module (see "Known gaps" below).
- `native/RooierbesturingNativeTests.cpp` -- the single AUnit driver
  (`setup()`/`loop()`), matching every other Triton module's `*NativeTests.cpp`
  pattern. Every `test_*.cpp` file only registers `test(...)` blocks; none of
  them define `setup()`/`loop()` themselves, so they all link into one
  combined binary instead of one binary per test file.
- `native/run_tests_msvc.ps1` -- MSVC fallback for Windows without GCC.

```
pio run -e native
.pio/build/native/program(.exe)
```

Needs a real host C++17 compiler on PATH (g++ or clang++) -- on Windows
without one, use test\native\run_tests_msvc.ps1 instead, which builds and
runs the same combined binary via MSVC directly. That script passes
`/Zc:preprocessor` -- MSVC's legacy preprocessor mishandles AUnit's
`test(SuiteName, testName)` argument-count dispatch, which every test in
this project uses.

## Known gaps

- Rooierbesturing's legacy source was not a complete, working program the
  way Ploegbesturing's/Pootmachinebesturing's/Spuitcomputer SP's
  were -- it was a patchwork of at least two incompatible drafts (a
  coherent class-based InterfaceRooier alongside an abandoned free-function
  updateMode() state machine), missing headers, wrong class names, and
  undeclared members throughout ImplementRooier. Reconstructed with intent
  where the source itself didn't resolve; see the distillation plan/commit
  message for the full account of what was kept, reconstructed, or dropped.
- **KP/KI/KD/manPwm/autoPwm are reconstructed, not ported.** No legacy
  fragment ever showed a working use of these declared-but-unused getters/
  setters. ImplementRooier now applies them as a real PID correction on top
  of the operator-set target height, modeled on ImplementPlanter's
  SetSetpoint()/Adjust() shape -- but this specific control algorithm has no
  legacy precedent and has not been validated against real windrower
  hardware. Defaults to kp=ki=kd=0 (inert: the PID path runs but contributes
  nothing) until a technician tunes it via CalibrationRooier's wizard.
- GPS was dropped for this module (`#ifdef GPS` was undefined by default in
  the legacy source too, and no fragment demonstrated a working GPS
  integration for windrower height control) -- tractor-only, matching
  Spuitcomputer SP's precedent. Unlike Spuitcomputer, no sibling
  module has a working GPS-fed version of *this* control scheme to point a
  future revival at, so no backlog item was added.
- `CalibrationRooier` (the LCD/button calibration wizard) is `#ifdef
  ARDUINO`-guarded and deliberately excluded from `[env:native]`'s
  `build_src_filter` -- same reason as `CalibrationPlough`/`CalibrationPlanter`/
  `CalibrationScraper`. No native coverage exists or is planned for it.
- `InterfaceRooier::UpdateScreen()` (LCD rendering) compiles as part of
  `InterfaceRooier.cpp` (needed since `CheckButtons()`/`Update()`, which
  *are* tested, live in the same file) but its output isn't asserted on by
  any test -- `test/native/support/InterfaceI2CLCD.hpp`'s `WriteBuffer`/
  `WriteScreen` are no-ops.
- The `teensy41` real-board env (in `src/main.cpp`) has not been
  build-verified on real hardware -- only via `pio run -e teensy41`'s
  cross-compile in this environment. Rooierbesturing also has no CAN/ISOBUS
  wiring at all (unlike Ploegbesturing/Pootmachinebesturing/Kilverbakbesturing),
  since it never used GPS/ISOBUS in the first place.
- Only the `teensy41` board is wired up today. This module's legacy source
  also referenced a production Arduino Micro board (its actual legacy
  default), an Arduino Micro/Leonardo "voorserie" preseries board, and a
  Teensy 3.5/3.6 protoboard -- their `#ifdef` branches remain in
  `ConfigRooier.hpp`/`ConfigImplementRooier.hpp`/`ConfigInterfaceRooier.hpp`
  for future revival, but none of them has a buildable `platformio.ini` env
  yet.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing (not used here -- pio test's
own Unity-based runner doesn't understand this project's AUnit setup):
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html
